NNAAMMEE
     ddaaeemmoonn — run in the background

LLIIBBRRAARRYY
     Standard C Library (libc, -lc)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ##iinncclluuddee <<ssttddlliibb..hh>>

     _i_n_t
     ddaaeemmoonn(_i_n_t _n_o_c_h_d_i_r, _i_n_t _n_o_c_l_o_s_e);

     _i_n_t
     ddaaeemmoonnffdd(_i_n_t _c_h_d_i_r_f_d, _i_n_t _n_u_l_l_f_d);

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddaaeemmoonn() function is for programs wishing to detach themselves from
     the controlling terminal and run in the background as system daemons.

     If the argument _n_o_c_h_d_i_r is zero, ddaaeemmoonn() changes the current working di‐
     rectory to the root (_/).

     If the argument _n_o_c_l_o_s_e is zero, ddaaeemmoonn() redirects standard input, stan‐
     dard output, and standard error to _/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l.

     The ddaaeemmoonnffdd() function is equivalent to the ddaaeemmoonn() function except
     that arguments are the descriptors for the current working directory and
     to the descriptor to _/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l.

     If _c_h_d_i_r_f_d is equal to (-1) the current working directory is not changed.

     If _n_u_l_l_f_d is equals to (-1) the redirection of standard input, standard
     output, and standard error is not closed.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ddaaeemmoonn() and ddaaeemmoonnffdd() functions return the value 0 if successful;
     otherwise the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set
     to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The ddaaeemmoonn() and ddaaeemmoonnffdd() function may fail and set _e_r_r_n_o for any of
     the errors specified for the library functions fork(2) open(2), and
     setsid(2).

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     fork(2), setsid(2), sigaction(2)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ddaaeemmoonn() function first appeared in 4.4BSD.  The ddaaeemmoonnffdd() function
     first appeared in FreeBSD 12.0.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     Unless the _n_o_c_l_o_s_e argument is non-zero, ddaaeemmoonn() will close the first
     three file descriptors and redirect them to _/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l.  Normally, these
     correspond to standard input, standard output, and standard error.  How‐
     ever, if any of those file descriptors refer to something else, they will
     still be closed, resulting in incorrect behavior of the calling program.
     This can happen if any of standard input, standard output, or standard
     error have been closed before the program was run.  Programs using
     ddaaeemmoonn() should therefore either call ddaaeemmoonn() before opening any files
     or sockets, or verify that any file descriptors obtained have values
     greater than 2.

     The ddaaeemmoonn() function temporarily ignores SIGHUP while calling setsid(2)
     to prevent a parent session group leader's calls to fork(2) and then
     _exit(2) from prematurely terminating the child process.

